    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "\"system of deliberate misdirection\"... yeah, that's a good way to phrase it for a formal report. \n\nthe reason i'm obsessing over this is because it feels less like a \"method\" and more like a personality trait shared by this whole batch of maps. it's like they were made by someone who just loves creating chaos and being difficult for the sake of it. it's hard to describe without sounding like i'm attributing a human-like, almost impish temperament to a group of anonymous cartographers. \n\ndo you think it's ever appropriate to use more \"character-driven\" descriptors in metadata, or is that a total bridge too far? like, could i ever justify tagging a collection as having a \"trickster\" or \"unpredictable\" provenance if the evidence of their behavior is so consistent across all the items? i just feel like \"intentional ambiguity\" is so clinical, and it doesn't quite capture the sense that these people were actively trying to mess with whoever came after them.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
